[{"code":"list_still_used","severity":"error","span":{"file":"tests/corpus/check/bad/inline/FunctionElementShared.beni","start":{"line":45,"col":10},"end":{"line":45,"col":17}},"title":"LIST CHANGED WHILE STILL IN USE","message":"`FunctionElementShared.shared` must return a list it owns, but\nFunctionElementShared.collect edits what it returns, which may be a list it does\nnot own.\n\n45|    case collect [ shared ] of\n            ^^^^^^^ the function is handed on here\n\nHave the function return a list it owns: a new list, or a copy made with\n`List.copy`.\n\nThis is sharing the checker can see: FunctionElementShared.collect edits what it\nreturns, which may be a list it does not own."},{"code":"top_level_list_changed","severity":"error","span":{"file":"tests/corpus/check/bad/inline/FunctionElementShared.beni","start":{"line":47,"col":13},"end":{"line":47,"col":27}},"title":"TOP-LEVEL LIST CHANGED","message":"`List.push` changes `xs` in place, but it is `kept`, a top-level value.\n\n47|            List.push xs 2\n               ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ the list is changed in place here\n\n36|kept = [ 1 ]\n   ^^^^ the top-level list is defined here\n\nA top-level value is shared by every part of the program and never changes:\nchanged in place, it would change for every part that reads it.\n\nMake `kept` a function that builds a fresh value at each call, or change a copy:\n`List.copy kept`.\n\nThis is the language's rule for top-level values, whether or not anything else\nreads the list (ownership-checker.md §4.2)."}]
